ement finished in 920s of its 2400s budget (exit 1).\nMeasure Component Disk Space\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-09-10T08:42:00.4885403Z ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.\nMeasure Component Disk Space\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-09-10T08:42:00.5022788Z ##[group]Run actions/upload-artifact@v7\nMeasure Component Disk Space\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-09-10T08:42:00.5023097Z with:\nMeasure Component Disk Space\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-09-10T08:42:00.5023310Z   name: disk-space-measurements\nMeasure Component Disk Space\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-09-10T08:42:00.5023644Z   path: data/disk-space-measurements.json\nMeasure Component Disk Space\tUNKNOWN STEP\tmeasurement.log\nMeasure Component Disk Space\tUNKNOWN STEP\t\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3240896Z     PASS: the…ary\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3241700Z     PASS: the…ere\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3242547Z     PASS: anc…nto\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3243430Z     PASS: the…lag\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3244357Z     PASS: the…epo\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3245053Z     PASS: the…out\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3245510Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3246073Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3247806Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3248741Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3249685Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3251049Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3252262Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3253269Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3254228Z     PASS: the…nk)\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3254988Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3255705Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3256443Z     PASS: the…nce\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3257460Z     PASS: [REDACTED] \"Errors per input\" heading is still parsed in full\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3258224Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3258844Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3259599Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3260281Z     PASS: [REDACTED]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3260805Z     --- the --include-fragments mutation fixture ---\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3261437Z     FAIL: with the flag, valid anchors are reported broken\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3262512Z     PASS: wit…ken\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3263504Z     PASS: wit…ken\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3264501Z     PASS: wit…hem\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3265405Z     note: set LINKS_LIVE=1 to run lychee for real (needs docker + network)\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3266307Z     \nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3267069Z     33 passed, 1 failed\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3267908Z ##[endgroup]\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3287956Z ##[error]Suite failed with exit status 1\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3295148Z ==> RUN  test-issue115-manifest-script.sh\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3295507Z ==> RUN  test-issue115-push-retry-classifier.sh\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:32.3295840Z ==> RUN  test-issue115-registry-split.sh\nscripts / regression suites\tUNKNOWN STEP\t2026-10-11T17:04:33.3441215Z ==> RUN  test-issue115-release-notes.sh\n83c067c 2.10.1: Keep the CI budget wrapper's private state outside temporary directories that the wrapped command may clean, and keep credentials out of opt-in verbose traces.\n41f76eb fix(ci): isolate budget wrapper state\nbc3cc33 2.10.0: Make every CI/CD check answer from the data it actually has (issue #123). The nine runs on `main` at `1d9fb3e` carry **804** lines matching `warn` or `error` and exactly **7** annotations; three of the seven said something untrue. **Eight of the nine runs were green**, and six of the twenty root causes are in those eight. One sentence covers all of them: a check that reported a verdict about data it never obtained. ### The red run was two false verdicts stacked `Measure Disk Space and Update README` annotated `…did not finish within its 2400s budget and was terminated` — and then ran **19 m 21 s** more, until `timeout-minutes: 60` cancelled it. `kill -0` exits 1 for both ESRCH (\"gone\") and EPERM (\"alive, not yours to signal\"), and the survivors were root-owned `apt-get` children of a `sudo`, so `run-with-budget-warning.sh` read them as finished, skipped its SIGKILL escalation, and exited 124 believing it had terminated the command. They also held the step's stdout, which is why the step could not end. Liveness now comes from the process table, escalation runs SIGTERM → SIGKILL → SIGKILL under `sudo`, any survivor is named in an `::error`, and the wrapper relays output instead of sharing a pipe. The gate then excused the cancellation as a supersede: `check-pipeline-status.sh` asked one question of the **run** and let the answer excuse every cancelled **job** in it, and `measure-disk-space.yml` declares `cancel-in-progress: false`, which a supersede cannot reach at all. `read-job-cancel-in-progress.sh` reads the effective value out of the workflow file `GITHUB_WORKFLOW_REF` names, and everything unreadable fails closed. Either verdict alone would have left a red run; together they produced a green one. ### The checks that could not fail zizmor printed `WARN audit: zizmor is running in offline mode` and `no findings to report` in the same green run: both passes were `docker run` without `-e GH_TOKEN`, so every online audit was skipped — `known-vulnerable-actions` among them, the one audit that can catch a supply-chain compromise of an action already trusted here. Measured over a `tj-actions/changed-files@v44` fixture: offline 7 findings, online 8, and the 2 `known-vulnerable-actions` hits appear only online. `run-zizmor.sh` pins image, floors and targets, passes the token by name and treats the offline banner as a failure. `if-no-files-found: warn` reported the absence of the only record of a run as a line in the log of the run whose record is missing; both uploads now ask `steps.<id>.outcome` and require files. `brew link … 2>&1 | grep -v Warning || true` tested grep's opinion of the text at four sites, hiding a failure that says why, a failure that says only `Warning:`, and issue #53's `timeout` 124. `npm --force` rode on the Playwright install since issue #84; twelve runs across both images, fresh and re-install, before and after `npm install -g npm@latest`, all exit 0 with the same version — the flag is gone. ### Text that was read as a verdict, and a log that was erased The runner's `ActionCommand.TryParse` accepts `##[` anywhere in a physical line, so `git commit` echoing a changeset that quotes `##[error]` becomes a `failure` annotation on a successful job. `run-with-commands-stopped.sh` brackets a child's output with `::stop-commands::<fresh 128-bit token>` and always resumes, at 15 call sites. Sweeping for those printers found `cmd 2>&1 | tee /dev/stderr`: `/dev/stderr` is `/proc/self/fd/2`, so `tee` **reopens** the file behind fd 2 with `O_TRUNC` — harmless when fd 2 is a pipe, destructive when it is a file. `capture-and-stream.sh` streams through a duplicate of the descriptor instead. ### `git diff` exiting 128 is not \"nothing changed\" Three release gates ran `git diff --name-only \"origin/$BASE...HEAD\"` and discarded the status: with no `fetch-depth: 0`, a renamed base or a failed fetch, the range does not resolve, the command prints nothing, and two of the three gates report the passing verdict on a branch that rewrote `VERSION` with no changeset. `scripts/release/pr-diff-range.sh` is the one place that asks what a pull request changed; it restores a base ref missing only locally and otherwise names the cause on stderr and returns 1. Extracting it surfaced that the changeset gate's path set omitted `VERSION`, `.github/actions/` and `.githooks/`, and echoed pull-request-controlled paths with command processing live. Two `gh` reads conflated \"empty\" with \"failed\"; the remaining **61** statements where a git or `gh` read could be mistaken for an answer — **27** in `scripts/`, `.github/` and `.githooks/` — are dispositioned one by one in `analysis/git-read-failure-sweep.md`, with the corrected query that finds them and the reason the first one could not. ### \"Nothing to check\" is not the same as \"could not look\" Eight `scripts/ci` gates discover their own inputs with `git ls-files`. Five ended that listing in `|| true` and the other three built it inside a process substitution; either way a git that could not answer — a busy index, an unreadable object, no `git` on `PATH` — arrived as an empty list, and the gate reported the empty list as a fact about the repository. Measured against the shipped `run-shellcheck.sh` with a `git` exiting 128: `==> No shell scripts to check`, status **0**, over 201 tracked shell scripts it never opened. All eight now separate \"git could not answer\" from \"git answered, and there is nothing\", and exit 2 naming which — as does `run-precommit-checks.sh`, the hook driver that runs them, which read the **index** the same way and printed `Nothing staged; no checks to run` over a commit it had not seen. The release path asked the same question of `VERSION`: 18 hand-rolled reads across six workflows, 16 of them behind `git pull origin main || true`, where an unreadable file leaves `version=` — a tag published as `ghcr.io/…/box-js:-amd64`, or a bump to `1.0.0` from whatever the version actually was. `scripts/release/release-version.sh` is the single reader now, it takes the version the pipeline already resolved (`fromJSON(inputs.changes)['version']`), and it refuses an empty one. The last one is a break this branch committed itself: three orphan `echo` lines left `release-full.yml` unparseable (`Psych::SyntaxError … line 175 column 33`), and all four gates that read workflow files exited 0 while describing its contents — each reads YAML with a regex or a `grep`, so a file that is not YAML at all is simply one they extract nothing from. `scripts/ci/check-workflow-yaml.sh` parses every workflow with a real parser before the others run. One step further out is the same question about a gate's *subject* rather than its input: `validate-changeset.sh` matched `.changeset/` at any depth, so this branch's own release run failed with `Invalid changeset format` over `dev/log/…/templates/go/.changeset/add-changeset-workflow.md` — a changeset belonging to another repository, pinned here as the evidence the issue asks for, written in the changesets format rather than this repository's `bump:` one. True about the file, false about this repository: `apply-changesets.sh` reads `find .changeset -maxdepth 1` and would never have applied it. The pattern is anchored at the root now, at the consumer's depth, and every gate that discovers its own inputs was re-measured against an evidence tree that holds seven other projects' source — 1355 inputs across ten gates, none of them under `dev/log/`. ### The shell a runner hands you is not the shell you tested in `tr: write error: Broken pipe` and `grep: write error: Broken pipe` on two passing jobs: a step's shell starts with SIGPIPE `SIG_IGN` ([actions/runner#2684](https://github.com/actions/runner/issues/2684), open since 2023), an ignored disposition survives `exec`, and bash cannot reset one — so `producer | head` gets EPIPE, a stderr line, and under `pipefail` a non-zero pipeline. Both sites bound the **reader** so the writer reaches EOF. The same runner difference is why `useradd -m` printed `the home directory /home/box already exists. Not copying any file from skel`: `WORKDIR /home/box` stands above it and Docker creates the directory, so five images shipped a `box` user with no `~/.profile` — the file that sources `~/.bashrc` for a login shell — and `ssh`, `su - box` and `bash -l` saw none of the PATH the install scripts append. ### Six defects of this issue's own class, in the instruments built to measure it Three of the twenty root causes are in code this branch wrote. A sweep matched its own fixture text; a measurement was read before the data was in; a comparison read `/tmp/roles-*.txt`, a glob that matched its own other output. Three more were found only by the GitHub runner, on suites green on every machine here: an assertion that apt's default retry count *equals* 3 (it is 3 here and in `ubuntu:24.04`, **1** on `ubuntu-24.04`, same apt 2.8.3 on the same Ubuntu 24.04.4 — and the invariant this repository depends on is that `-o Acquire::Retries=3` is never a *downgrade*, which is a direction, not an equality); a \"SIGPIPE at its default\" leg that inherited the runner's `SIG_IGN` instead of establishing anything, making both legs the same leg; and a fake `ps` whose unkillable survivor died with the process group it was standing in for. All six are fixed at the root — a premise read out of `/proc/self/status`, a fixture that outlives what it stands in for, a private `mktemp -d` — never by loosening an assertion. ### Evidence, tests and what went upstream `dev/log/issues/123/pulls/124/` holds 7.4 MB of it: all nine run logs and all 99 jobs of the release run, the 804-line census with every line classified, all 7 annotations, the zizmor/apt/useradd/npm probes, a nine-document analysis, and the full role matrix of this repository's CI tree against all seven `link-foundation/*-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template` repositories, file by file, with 91 gaps dispositioned individually. Six defect classes found in that comparison were filed upstream as **19 issues and 1 comment** across six template repositories, each with a reproduction that runs offline, a workaround and a fix in diff form. Fifteen new offline suites, **551 assertions, 0 failures**, each checker exercised in a passing *and* a failing form and every sweep pinned to a site count. `PR_DIFF_RANGE_VERBOSE=1` (or `BOX_VERBOSE=1`) traces how a pull request's diff range was computed — default off, on stderr — and the apt suite prints its measured default, `apt-config dump`, every apt.conf line setting retries with its contents and whether `apt-get` is a wrapper, and fails if the dumped value and the measured default disagree, so the one question the evidence cannot answer today arrives explained on the next run. `docs/case-studies/issue-123/CASE-STUDY.md` has the whole analysis.\n8b4941e Make the apt diagnostic print values, not names, and self-check (issue #123)\n0cbd87c Number and narrate the twentieth root cause (issue #123)\n2ba6591 Make six more checks answer from the data they have (issue #123)\n1a756e6 docs: add the issue #123 case study\n19a377f Record the warning/error census and the best-practices re-measurement (issue #123)\na2c97c2 Compare box's whole CI file tree against all seven templates (issue #123)\n09549d9 Give the box user the home directory useradd refused to make (issue #123)\nc151dd5 fix: give brew link back its own exit status at all four sites (issue #123)\n2b9fa1e fix: stop disabling npm's protections for an install that never needed it (issue #123)\ncd25f22 fix: make both artifact uploads fail when the evidence is missing (issue #123)\nd239e5b upstream: file the six defect classes issue #123 found in the templates\ne3a9648 Retire the apt \"hardening\" item by measuring apt's own defaults (issue #123)\n0663aa2 Record the zizmor offline-mode measurements as issue evidence (issue #123)\n82656ba Give zizmor a token, and fail the run when it says it is offline (issue #123)\n6a46916 ci: ask each cancelled job whether a supersede could have cancelled it (issue #123)\n4139d54 Stop untrusted text from becoming annotations, and stop `tee /dev/stderr` from erasing the log (issue #123)\neacba67 ci: make a budget that reports termination actually terminate (issue #123)\n1e202f5 2.9.0: Make every CI annotation mean what it says, and give every check a way to fail (issue #121). The eight runs the issue lists carried **98 annotations** — 58 `failure`, 29 `warning`, 11 `notice` — and seven of those eight runs were green. Two of the 98 were the run that was actually red, and they were reporting a test assertion that could only pass on 2026-09-08. ### The 56 failures a commit message printed Release run 34293699247 finished with every build job green and 56 `failure` annotations carrying the body of commit `a2e6420`, which quotes `##[error]` while explaining a fix. `docker/setup-buildx-action` creates a `docker-container` builder, and since buildx v0.30.0 that driver bakes the whole `GITHUB_EVENT_PATH` payload into the builder at **create** time; `provenance: false` drops the image attestation but not the metadata file, whose default `min` mode keeps `invocation.environment.github_event_payload`; `docker/build-push-action` prints that file with `core.info(JSON.stringify(...))`, so a whole commit message arrives as one physical line; and the runner's `ActionCommand.TryParse` accepts `##[` **anywhere** in a line, unlike `TryParseV2`. So any commit message quoting `##[error]` annotates the next release, and `stop-commands` is in the same registered set. `BUILDX_METADATA_PROVENANCE: disabled` at workflow scope in all six building workflows removes the payload rather than the printing. Filed as docker/buildx#4066, docker/build-push-action#1612 and actions/runner#4692, each with the reproduction, a workaround and a fix in diff form. ### The 28 warnings that were also a false negative `jlumbroso/free-disk-space` removes a fixed list of package names. Google publishes no arm64 apt repository, so on `ubuntu-24.04-arm` apt stops at `E: Unable to locate package google-chrome-stable`, exits 100 — and removes **none** of the other five in the same command. The annotation reported the absence of a package that architecture never had, and was simultaneously the only notice that every arm64 job was keeping the packages the reclaim existed to take. `scripts/ci/reclaim-large-packages.sh` hands apt the intersection of the action's own patterns with what `dpkg-query` reports installed, so a failure now means something installed could not be removed. ### The checks that could not fail `.hadolint.yaml` failed at `warning` while all ten findings in the repository are `info`, and the runner mapped annotation levels beside a threshold it never read. All ten are resolved — DL3015 per site, measured by resolving each apt plan twice in `ubuntu:24.04`, so `--no-install-recommends` is added where it is a no-op and refused where the recommends are the product — and the threshold is hadolint's default now. `zizmor` scanned `.github/workflows` and nothing else, so the four composite actions had never been read by any linter; widening it surfaced a High-confidence `template-injection` in `dockerhub-login`. The `'*': hash-pin` policy could not apply to images at all, because those audits are Pedantic-only: a second high/high pass restores it, and `docker://rhysd/actionlint` is pinned by digest — which then froze the wrong thing, because a digest pin freezes the check set and not just the binary. The tag resolved to v1.7.7, and the three checks it cannot make each report a workflow that never runs: `glob` (a `paths:` entry beginning with `./` matches nothing), `if-cond` (`if: false` on a job still in the status gate's `needs`) and `runner-label` (a label GitHub has removed). Over one fixture per check plus a YAML merge key, v1.7.7 reports one finding and describes it wrongly; v1.7.12 reports four. The pin is v1.7.12, the floor is asserted offline against the version trailer, and `test-issue115-ci-policy.sh` — which had been running the mutable tag while the workflow used a digest — reads the image out of the workflow now. `playwright install` printed a host-validation warning on both JS build jobs and exited 0, shipping browsers linked against libraries the image lacks — seven packages short of Playwright's own list; all seven are installed and the warning is a build failure now. The same defect turned up in an assertion written *for* this branch: it extracted a `run:` block with `awk '/^\\s+run: \\|/,0'`, and `\\s` is a GNU extension — under mawk, which is what Debian and Ubuntu point `awk` at, it matches nothing and is not diagnosed, so the range never opens and the assertion passes on any input; under gawk, which the runner ships, `,0` never closes, because no record is ever numbered 0, so the “block” runs to end of file and correct `with:` mappings are reported as injections. The block is bounded by indentation now, and `scripts/ci/check-awk-portability.sh` fails the run on any GNU-only escape in any awk program in any tracked file. And the same question asked of the JavaScript here found ten of the fourteen tracked modules — the ranking fetchers and the `.mjs` probes — parsed by nothing at all, with two of the remaining four executed only on the path where the links check has already failed. `scripts/ci/check-mjs-syntax.sh`, ported from the js template, parses every one of them and resolves every relative import. ### Grey is not red, and a backstop is not a deadline GitHub ranks a cancellation above a failure when it folds job conclusions into a run conclusion, so run 34259552358 concluded grey while `pr-test / dind-full` inside it had concluded `failure` 26 minutes earlier. `scripts/ci/check-pipeline-status.sh` is now a terminal gate in every entry-point workflow — a failure is always an error, a cancellation is an error only when the run is still the head of its branch — and `check-status-gate-covers-all-jobs.mjs` fails CI when a job is added outside a gate. One level down, a job killed by `timeout-minutes` names neither the step nor the number: 22 long steps are wrapped in `run-with-budget-warning.sh`, every cap is sized from `measure-job-durations.sh` (the release builds carried 120 minutes against a measured maximum of 35.6; `measure-disk-space` carried 180 against 23.3), and `check-timeout-budgets.mjs` keeps each budget under 70% of its cap, per matrix leg. ### The false positives that had no cheap fix lychee answers `false` for the connect phase, so `--max-retries` never applies to a connection reset and the links gate fails on a healthy host; the cheap fix, an `.lycheeignore` entry, converts that into a permanent false negative. `scripts/ci/recheck-broken-links.mjs` re-asks only the URLs no host answered. Three jobs wrote to `main` with a bare `git push origin main`, and a ruleset rejection prints the same word as a race — classify the rule as a race and the job rebases, is declined identically, and blames a race that never happened. `git-push-failure-classifier.sh` tests for a rule first, and `git-push-with-retry.sh` answers a rule with a pull request. ### The documentation nothing validated `links.yml` has resolved every URL in the tracked markdown since issue #115 — the *document*, never the anchor. `docs/RELEASING.md#making-the-ghcr-packages-public` passed because that file existed, whatever headings it contained, and an anchor is the part of a link a heading rename breaks; 27 links in the corpus carry one. `--include-fragments` is the whole fix, and a mutation fixture pins that it is: the same two files with both target headings renamed report `2 OK 🚫 0 Errors` without the flag and `Cannot find fragment` twice with it. Turning it on found 4 errors on 3 URLs — a Docker page that was restructured, a README heading that was reworded, a GitHub docs page that moved — each confirmed dead in a browser and repointed; the full corpus is then 681 links, 667 OK, 0 errors in 14.6 s, so the flag costs no false positives and no extra requests. No link checker can ask the other half. `update-readme-sizes.sh` inserts the generated component-sizes section before README's `## License`, falling back to `## Documentation`, and `measure-disk-space` commits the result to `main`: rename either heading and the table is appended to the end of the file with the job green. `preflight-credentials.sh` sends an operator holding a rejected credential to \"the 'Releasing' section of README.md\", and two more scripts name `docs/RELEASING.md` — strings read on the worst day of a release, pointing at headings nothing verified. `scripts/ci/check-required-docs.sh`, ported from the js template, checks six documents, the 24 headings the code depends on, both component-sizes markers, and every `docs/…md` path named anywhere in `scripts/` or `.github/`. It runs in a new `docs.yml`, whose triggers are the union of the markdown and the shell — which in turn exposed that `test-issue115-fresh-merge.sh` could only see workflows reachable from the release entry point, so a new standalone check workflow could skip the fresh-merge action unnoticed; it now derives the list from the workflow directory. ### The cheapest place to find any of this Fifteen of the templates' sixteen best practices were already held here; the sixteenth, pre-commit hooks, was recorded as not adopted because `install-git-hooks.mjs` drives husky and there is no `package.json` at this root. That was right about the tool and wrong about the practice. `.githooks/pre-commit` delegates to `scripts/ci/run-precommit-checks.sh`, which runs the same thirteen gates CI runs — shfmt, shellcheck, heredoc-vars, awk-portability, mjs-syntax, py-syntax, status-gate coverage, timeout budgets, path coverage, checkout credentials, required-docs, file-line-limits, secretlint — scoped to what is staged: about 13 s for a commit touching shell, about 9 for markdown only. It checks the **index**, not the working tree, because `git commit` records the index and a hook that reads the worktree is wrong in both directions — passing a commit whose fix is unstaged, failing a commit whose breakage is unstaged. The gates run in a throwaway `git checkout-index` mirror (0.9 s to write 774 files, 2.8 s including the mirror's own index), built with `add -A -f` because without `-f` the `*.log` line of `.gitignore` silently drops ten tracked files from every checker's view. It rewrites nothing, a gate that cannot run says so and does not block, and a suite assertion requires every gate the hook runs to also be run by a workflow, so that tolerance can never become a hole. `scripts/install-git-hooks.sh` takes what the template actually knows — husky \"exits 0 even when it fails\" — and verifies the outcome: it reads `core.hooksPath` back and stats the hook, and fails if either disagrees. Its first run over its own commit found a false positive that had been latent since the checker was written: `check-heredoc-vars.sh` collected one name per `export` statement, so `export RECORD=… EXITS=…` left `EXITS` looking unset and any heredoc reading it was reported as a leak. It now walks the whole statement, tracking quote state, and stops at the first token that is not a name so `export -f` still registers nothing. ### Evidence and tests Every number above comes out of `dev/log/issues/121/pulls/122/`: all 98 annotations, the 200-run conclusion survey, the job logs, the provenance probes, and the bodies of the seven upstream reports behind the eight issues filed — docker/buildx#4066 and #4067, docker/build-push-action#1612, actions/runner#4692, docker/setup-buildx-action#615, js-template#184 with rust-template#170, and js-template#185, the one the template comparison itself produced. That comparison is finished file by file and recorded with it: every remaining divergence — the templates' `dependency-review` (box tracks zero dependency manifests, so it would be another check that cannot fail), their npm release path, their `lint-changed-lines.mjs` — is declined in writing with the measurement behind the decision, and the three invariants every job is supposed to carry were re-measured across all 127 jobs in the three repositories. That last sweep produced three false positives of my own — `timeout-minutes` on jobs where GitHub rejects the key, a `permissions:` count that ignored the workflow-level default, a `concurrency:` count that ignored both the call graph and the status gate's deliberate omission — each retired by verification rather than encoded as a gate. `docs/case-studies/issue-121/CASE-STUDY.md` has the full analysis; `docs/CI-TIMEOUT-BUDGETS.md` the measured caps. 838 new offline assertions across twenty suites — `git-hooks` (91), `playwright-deps` (89), `git-init-hint` (83), `pipeline-status-gate` (72), `checkout-credentials` (67), `reclaim-large-packages` (47), `links-recheck` (39), `timeout-budgets` (39), `mjs-syntax` (38), `required-docs` (38), `path-coverage` (37), `awk-portability` (35), `git-push-recovery` (33), `py-syntax` (26), `readme-updater` (25), `log-injection` (23), `workflow-audit-scope` (23), `clock-independence` (12), `provenance-metadata-leak` (11), `buildx-cleanup` (10) — each checker exercised in both a passing and a failing form, and 61 more in ten suites that already existed: 13 in `test-issue115-heredoc-unbound-vars.sh` for the multi-name `export`, 9 in the hadolint gate, 8 each in the shfmt and shellcheck gates, 7 each in `test-issue115-line-limits.sh` and `test-issue115-experiment-runner.sh`, 6 in `test-issue115-links-gate.sh` for the fragment flag (four of which drive lychee itself), and one each in the fresh-merge, image-tags and parallel-PR-test suites. The hook's own suite stubs every gate as a recorder, so it asserts which gate saw which content; four mutations of the shipped scripts each produce exactly one intended failure.\n7f2fb1e docs: re-measure every number this branch quotes about itself (issue #121)\na210b1c ci: pin the actionlint that can report a workflow which never runs (issue #121)\ne724270 ci: make every checkout say what happens to the job token, and check that it matches (issue #121)\ne3563aa ci: measure how long each job keeps the token checkout leaves behind (issue #121)\nf0e756c ci: make a gate's inputs able to start the workflow that runs it, and make discovery answer about the repository (issue #121)\n5c2b8f6 ci: run CI's own gates before the commit exists (issue #121)\nf97a54b ci: check the headings the code reads at runtime (issue #121)\nbe82a89 ci: render the README table into this run's own file, and write the file that was named (issue #121)\nb8ef077 ci: parse every JavaScript module the repository ships (issue #121)\n5396265 chore: add the changeset the release gate asks for (issue #121)\ne95614a ci: give every long step a deadline it can report itself (issue #121)\ne824d9c docs: file the template all_recovered false negative upstream (issue #121)\n557add3 ci: give every workflow a terminal gate, and check that it covers every job (issue #121)\n0c9aa1b ci: classify a rejected push before retrying it, and stop rebasing at a rule (issue #121)\ncd1ba48 ci: audit the composite actions and enforce the image-pinning policy that had no auditor (issue #121)\nd2d3f7e ci: fail on the Playwright warning both JS jobs printed, and resolve the ten hadolint findings that could not fail (issue #121)\n42858af 2.8.0: Publish every architecture the release built, and fail when a tag serves fewer (issue #119). Run 34056619231 finished `success` and left `konard/box:latest` amd64-only — the tag the README hands to every reader, which had been multi-arch since June. `docker manifest inspect` still answered 200, so nothing noticed. Four mechanisms, one sentence: **\"it resolves\" is not \"it was published\"**. ### The two tools could never have agreed `mirror-to-dockerhub.sh` copies with `docker buildx imagetools create`, which always wraps its result in an OCI index — even around a single platform. `create-multiarch-manifest.sh` then fed those copies to `docker manifest create`, which refuses an index source: `docker.io/***/box:2.7.0-amd64 is a manifest list`, three times, 40 seconds, then a warning. Retrying a deterministic failure is not resilience, it is a permanent defect wearing a transient one's costume — which is why the warning read like a flaky registry for a month. The manifest step publishes with `imagetools create` now, and Docker Hub assembles nothing at all: it mirrors the index GHCR already serves, which is what the issue proposed. ### Which job may write a tag a user pulls The `box` amd64 job mirrored **8** tags, the four suffixed and the four unsuffixed, so an architecture-specific job claimed `:latest`; `box-dind`'s mirror wrote only suffixed tags, which is why `konard/box-dind:2.7.0` is simply absent. Two spellings of one step, disagreeing about a rule nobody had written down. It is written down now — only a manifest step may name a bare tag — and `experiments/test-issue119-tag-policy.sh` reads every image reference out of the five release workflows and asserts it, so the next family added to the matrix cannot pick the other convention. For the manifest step to be the only writer it has to know every tag the release writes, which it did not: `docker/metadata-action` evaluates `{{date 'YYYYMMDD'}}` when the step runs, and the full box takes over an hour to build, so the two architecture jobs of a release started near midnight UTC name different tags — and the date and commit tags stayed single-architecture (`ghcr.io/link-foundation/box:20260907 linux/amd64`). `scripts/release/image-tags.sh` computes the list once and hands it to the other jobs, so every tag of a release reaches the manifest step. ### The check now asks what a tag serves `check-publication.sh` asked whether a reference resolves, and an amd64-only index answers yes. It reads the platform list now and fails on a reference carrying less than the release built, holding three distinctions apart: a **missing** mirror tag is lag and stays a warning (a mirror may trail the registry of record), a mirror tag that **resolves with the wrong architectures** is an error because somebody pulling it today gets the wrong answer today, and an **unreadable** platform list means \"I could not look\" — never \"one architecture\", which would be issue #117's false claim pointed the other way, at the cost of a release. `EXPECTED_PLATFORMS` is a constant, so `PREVIOUS_VERSION` adds the comparison a constant cannot make: a tag that was multi-arch in the previous release and is single-arch now is a regression regardless. `latest` joined the sample, being the tag the regression landed on. ### The tables say what was measured The v2.7.0 notes listed `konard/box-dind:2.7.0` as not published and then linked its `2.7.0-arm64` tag, under a column headed **Multi-arch**, while `konard/box:2.7.0` served linux/amd64 alone. A heading reads the same in the release where it is false — the same defect as a check that cannot fail, in documentation form. The heading is \"Tag\", the cell carries the probe's answer for that exact reference (`**linux/amd64 only**, missing linux/arm64`), and a per-architecture tag is linked only where its parent index was measured to serve that platform. Without `VERIFY_IMAGES` nothing was measured and the notes say so instead of implying it. The README's tables follow, and now state what the registries actually carried on 2026-09-08. ### The two CI jobs that could not finish `pr-test / full` and `pr-test / dind-full` — the only two jobs that hold JS, essentials, 11 language images and the full box on one runner — died four times in the full box's `#64 exporting layers`, with `exit code 143` and `The runner has received a shutdown signal`, while every other job passed on the same commit. Nothing in either job had ever sampled a resource, so `scripts/ci/resource-monitor.sh` now prints memory, disk, swap and the biggest processes from *inside* the build step, which is the only output that survives a runner going down. It answered on the next run: 11 MB of disk consumed across the whole 4.5-minute export with 88 GB still free, against 2.3 GB → **18.8 GB of committed memory** still climbing when the runner was taken down. dockerd buffering an export in anonymous memory is [docker/buildx#1606](https://github.com/docker/buildx/issues/1606), open since Docker 23.0 with nothing to configure; what changed on our side is that commit 46f80a5 made the Lean boxes install a real toolchain, so the full box's `COPY --from=lean-stage` stopped copying a stub. 16 GB of RAM is what an `ubuntu-24.04` runner has and larger runners are not available here, so `scripts/ci/ensure-swap.sh` spends the idle disk on the resource that ran out: a 32 GB swap target, sized down only if it would encroach on the 40 GB the chain and its export still need, decided out loud on `[ensure-swap]` lines, and never a job failure — a runner that cannot get swap gets a warning and the build attempt, because the failure belongs on the build. The `full` dind variant gets 90 minutes instead of 60, since an export that pages is slower than one that does not, and a timeout would hide the fix. ### Evidence and tests Run anonymously against the released v2.7.0 from this branch, the new gate exits 1 with `::error title=The Docker Hub mirror of v2.7.0 is single-architecture` — GHCR 8/8 pullable and multi-arch, Docker Hub 5/8 with `konard/box:2.7.0` and `:latest` at the same amd64-only digest. The transcript, the regenerated notes and the per-reference platform sweeps are in `dev/log/issues/119/pulls/120/`; `docs/case-studies/issue-119/` has the full analysis. 170 offline assertions across seven suites: `test-issue115-release-notes.sh` (50), `test-issue119-ci-resource-headroom.sh` (40, with the swapfile's privileged steps stubbed so the sequence is checked without root), `test-issue119-platform-coverage.sh` (21), `test-issue119-image-tags.sh` (19), `test-issue119-check-publication.sh` (16), `test-issue119-tag-policy.sh` (13) and `test-issue119-manifest-media-type.sh` (11). ### Still outstanding Nothing here rewrites a tag that is already published, so the Docker Hub mirror of 2.7.0 stays wrong until a release runs with these changes. GHCR — the registry of record — has been correct throughout, including for the consumer that prompted the issue.\n.github/workflows/measure-disk-space.yml:44:      verbose:\n.github/workflows/measure-disk-space.yml:165:      BOX_VERBOSE: ${{ inputs.verbose && '1' || '0' }}\n.github/workflows/measure-disk-space.yml:202:          echo \"=== Disk space after cleanup ===\"\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:8:# Usage: ./measure-disk-space.sh [--json-output FILE] [--verbose]\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:12:#   --verbose           trace what the script resolves, generates and hands to\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:39:    -v | --verbose)\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:86:# Off unless --verbose / BOX_VERBOSE=1. This job runs unattended for ~40\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:162:apt_update_with_retry() {\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:202:cleanup_for_measurement() {\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:209:  maybe_sudo rm -rf /tmp/* 2>/dev/null || true\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:210:  maybe_sudo rm -rf /var/tmp/* 2>/dev/null || true\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:284:  cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:293:    cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:302:    # Handle negative values (can happen due to cleanup removing more than installed)\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:345:cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:385:apt_update_with_retry || true\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:386:cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:519:cleanup_for_measurement() {\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:522:  rm -rf /tmp/measure-* 2>/dev/null || true\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:560:  cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:565:    cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:693:cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:695:  cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:780:cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:782:  cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:956:rm -f /tmp/box-measure.sh\nscripts/measure-disk-space.sh:963:cleanup_for_measurement\nscripts/ci/run-with-budget-warning.sh:85:#                           RUNNER_TEMP in GitHub Actions, then TMPDIR or /tmp)\nscripts/ci/run-with-budget-warning.sh:118:verbose=\"${BUDGET_VERBOSE:-0}\"\nscripts/ci/run-with-budget-warning.sh:131:trace() { [ \"${verbose}\" = \"1\" ] && echo \"[budget] $*\" >&2 || true; }\nscripts/ci/run-with-budget-warning.sh:138:# separation; TMPDIR remains the portable local fallback.\nscripts/ci/run-with-budget-warning.sh:139:state_parent=\"${BUDGET_STATE_PARENT:-${RUNNER_TEMP:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}}\"\nscripts/ubuntu-24-server-install.sh:9:# Usage: ./ubuntu-24-server-install.sh [--verbose]\nscripts/ubuntu-24-server-install.sh:11:#   --verbose  trace what the script resolves, generates and hands to the box\nscripts/ubuntu-24-server-install.sh:20:    -v | --verbose)\nscripts/ubuntu-24-server-install.sh:81:# Debug output, silent unless --verbose / BOX_VERBOSE=1 (issue #115).\nscripts/ubuntu-24-server-install.sh:167:apt_update_with_retry() {\nscripts/ubuntu-24-s